## 2. Tracking Shell Caret/Backslash Injections (Windows & Linux/macOS)

* Windows CMD Rule: Look for high densities of the caret character. Flag any command line input where the ratio of carets to regular letters exceeds a specific baseline (e.g., ^p^i^n^g).
* Linux/macOS Rule: Monitor for process arguments containing unexpected combinations of backslashes or quotation marks inside standard path environments (e.g., s\l\e\e\p).

## 3. Parent-Child Process Monitoring (The Ultimate Safety Net)
Regardless of how heavily an attacker obfuscates their initial text input inside your authentication payload, the final system execution cannot be hidden.

* The Rule: Monitor the backend application process (e.g., node, python, w3wp.exe, php-fpm).
* The Alert: Trigger an immediate critical alert if your web server process directly spawns a system shell binary such as /bin/sh, /bin/zsh, cmd.exe, or powershell.exe. Web applications should never spin up command shells to process a basic user login.

